epair: make a retry RECORDABLE through the public surface, while keeping every existing law:\na second `record_attempt` on a record whose state is `ATTEMPTED`, `OBSERVED`, `READBACK`,\n`UNKNOWN_EFFECT` or `PARTIAL_EFFECT` is accepted when its `attempt_key` is NEW, moves the record back\nto `ATTEMPTED` (a fresh attempt supersedes the previous observation for reconciliation purposes) and\nappends to `attempts`; a second attempt with an EXISTING `attempt_key` still raises\n`DuplicateAttemptDetected`; a terminal state other than `UNKNOWN_EFFECT`/`PARTIAL_EFFECT` still refuses\n(typed). Add tests that reach `plan_retry` -> `record_attempt` -> `reconcile` end-to-end through the\npublic API, plus a negative case that a `RECONCILED` record cannot be retried.\n\n## F3 — `src/assurance/evidence.py`: `invalidate()` direction is inverted for two identity axes\n\n`invalidate()` uses `bound != changed` for `subject_sha` but `bound == changed` for `oracle_id` and\n`environment`, so it invalidates the gates already bound to the NEW identity and leaves the superseded\nones live.\n\nRequired repair: use ONE direction for every identity axis — a gate is invalidated when the identity it\nwas bound to is NO LONGER the current one (i.e. `bound != changed`), including when the gate carries no\nbinding for that axis (then it is not invalidated by that axis). Add a test with two gates (one bound to\nthe superseded oracle/environment, one bound to the current) asserting exactly the superseded one is\ninvalidated, and a test that an unrelated axis change invalidates nothing.\n\n## F4 — `src/assurance/oracle.py`: the negative-fixture law is satisfiable by the item under check\n\nThe lane found that \"negative fixtures must be represented\" reads a self-declared field on the item\nitself, so one item that merely LISTS the fixture string makes the oracle PASS with no negative proof.\n\nRequired repair: the representation set must be derived from the EVIDENCE (the kinds/ids actually\npresent in the checked items, matched against a plan-level declaration), not from a field the item\ndeclares about itself. An item that declares its own `negative_fixtures` must no longer be able to\nsatisfy the law. Add a test proving the self-declared form does NOT pass and that a genuinely\nrepresented fixture does.\n\n## F5 — `src/assurance/journal.py`: delete and naive reorder collapse to the same reason\n\n`verify()` reports `SEQ_GAP` for BOTH a deleted entry and a naive swap of two entries, so the required\n\"distinct reason\" clause fails; the documented `PREV_DIGEST_MISMATCH` only appears after renumbering.\n\nRequired repair: detect a reorder from the `prev_digest` LINKAGE independently of `seq`, so that\n(i) payload mutation -> `ENTRY_DIGEST_MISMATCH`, (ii) deletion/truncation -> `SEQ_GAP`,\n(iii) reordering -> `PREV_DIGEST_MISMATCH`, (iv) foreign chain -> `CHAIN_ID_MISMATCH`,\n(v) header tampering -> `HEADER_MISMATCH`, and an untouched chain -> intact. Add one test per case\nasserting the DISTINCT reason code, and keep the docstring consistent with the code (state the rule as\na behavioural description; do not write \"exact description of the implementation\").\n\n## Definition of done\n\nAll four affected roots must pass:\n\n```\npython -m unittest discover -s src/security/tests    -t src/security/tests\npython -m unittest discover -s src/effect/tests      -t src/effect/tests\npython -m unittest discover -s src/assurance/tests   -t src/assurance/tests\n```\n\nwith counts strictly GREATER than the pre-repair counts (159 / 91 / 95) — a repair that does not move\nthe collected count means the new test was not collected. Print a JSON object when finished:\n`{\"files\": [...], \"counts\": {\"security\": N, \"effect\": N, \"assurance\": N}}`.\n\r\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
